  "activity": {
    "id": "act_retrograde_motion",
    "name": "Understanding Apparent Retrograde Motion",
    "description": "A seven-step exploration of why outer planets appear to reverse direction in the night sky. Students observe Mars's retrograde loop from Earth, examine the geocentric epicycle explanation, discover the heliocentric resolution, link the two viewpoints, embody the overtaking effect, compare outer planets, and recap the core concepts.",
    "workplace": "wp_retrograde_motion",
    "start": "step_observe",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_observe",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Puzzle in the Night Sky",
          "description": "Look up at the starfield. Watch Mars (the red point) drift eastward against the constellations of the ecliptic, then slow, reverse into a westward loop of about 15 degrees, and finally resume eastward motion. The trail marks its complete path. This backward motion was the central puzzle of pre-modern astronomy. Click Next when you have seen the full loop."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_nightsky",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_mars_retrograde_loop",
              "poi": "poi_ecliptic_band"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_nightsky",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_mars",
              "poi": "poi_ecliptic_band",
              "text": "Mars"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_nightsky",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_intro_puzzle",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_nightsky",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_geocentric"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_geocentric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Geocentric Explanation",
          "description": "Earth sits at the center. Mars rides on a small circle (the epicycle) that itself rides on a larger circle (the deferent). Watch the machinery run: when Mars reaches the inner part of its epicycle it appears to move backward as seen from Earth. This reproduces the loop, but only by carefully tuning epicycle and deferent sizes and speeds \u2014 and better data forced astronomers to stack even more epicycles. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_geocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_geocentric_epicycle",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_geocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_epicycle",
              "poi": "poi_epicycle",
              "text": "Epicycle"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_geocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_deferent",
              "poi": "poi_deferent",
              "text": "Deferent"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_geocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_geocentric",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_geocentric_model",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_heliocentric"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_heliocentric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Heliocentric Resolution",
          "description": "Now view the solar system from above. The Sun sits at the center; Earth orbits at 1 AU (one year) and Mars at 1.52 AU (1.88 years). Watch Earth catch up to and overtake slower-moving Mars roughly every 26 months. The retrograde loop disappears \u2014 there are no epicycles, just two smooth ellipses. The 'backward' motion is simply the effect of Earth passing Mars. Click Next to link this to the night sky."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_heliocentric_orbits",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_sun",
              "poi": "poi_sun",
              "text": "Sun"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_heliocentric",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_link"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_link",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Linking the Two Views",
          "description": "See the heliocentric model and Earth's night sky side by side. A line of sight is drawn from Earth through Mars out to the background stars. As Earth approaches Mars the line sweeps eastward; as Earth overtakes Mars the line reverses and sweeps westward \u2014 retracing Mars's apparent path. After Earth passes, the line resumes eastward. The retrograde loop is a real apparent motion produced by geometry, not an illusion of the eye. Click Next to try it yourself."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_heliocentric_orbits",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_lineofsight_link",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_nightsky",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_mars_retrograde_loop",
              "poi": "poi_ecliptic_band"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_nightsky",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_mars",
              "poi": "poi_ecliptic_band",
              "text": "Mars"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_nightsky",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_walk"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_walk",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Walk the Overtaking Effect",
          "description": "Stand on the orbit floor circle with virtual Mars placed ahead of you against the distant stars. Walk slowly around the circle \u2014 this is Earth's orbit \u2014 keeping your gaze on Mars. As you pass the point of closest approach, watch Mars appear to move backward against the stars. You have personally generated the retrograde pattern with your own motion. Complete the loop, then the step will advance when the floor circle is detected as fully traversed."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_floor",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_walk_orbit_guide",
              "poi": "poi_walk_path"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_floor",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_mars",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Mars (ahead)"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_orbit_floor",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "det_orbit_floor_marker",
            "duration": 3000
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_compare",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Comparing the Outer Planets",
          "description": "Return to the heliocentric view, now with Earth, Mars, Jupiter (5.2 AU), and Saturn (9.5 AU). Step through a retrograde event for each. Notice that Mars's loop is about 15 degrees, Jupiter's about 10 degrees, and Saturn's about 7 degrees. More distant planets show smaller loops because Earth's orbital motion subtends a smaller angle when viewed from farther away \u2014 the same parallax geometry that makes nearby objects shift more than distant ones when you move your head. Click Next for the recap."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_outer_planet_compare",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_sun",
              "poi": "poi_sun",
              "text": "Sun"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_compare",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_model",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_recap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Recap of Retrograde Motion",
          "description": "Review the five concept areas covered in this lab: (1) the observed phenomenon of Mars's loop, (2) the geocentric explanation with epicycles, (3) the heliocentric resolution, (4) the linkage between the two viewpoints, and (5) the comparison across outer planets. Each area is highlighted in turn on the summary board. The heliocentric model is preferred because it explains all of this with simple geometry and no ad hoc machinery \u2014 a triumph of parsimony. Click Done to finish."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_summary_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "img_summary_board",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_summary_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_summary",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_summary_board",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_recap"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
